/-!
# Headline statements of undergraduate-math-lean 0.2.2: topology (Morris, *Topology Without Tears*)

The main results of this topic that Mathlib and Tau Ceti lack, one theorem each, with every proof a deliberate
`sorry`: the period three theorem; Peano's space-filling curve onto the `n`-cube; the Banach–Mazur theorem (every
separable normed space embeds isometrically in `C([0, 1])`); the structure of closed subgroups of `ℝⁿ`, of the
quotients of `ℝⁿ` and of closed subgroups of the torus; the Wallman compactification and its coincidence with the
Stone–Čech compactification exactly for normal spaces; and Pontryagin–van Kampen duality for the groups
`ℝᵃ × 𝕋ᵇ × ℤᶜ`; and, added in 0.2.1: Pontryagin–van Kampen duality for every locally compact Hausdorff abelian
group; Sharkovskii's theorem on the periods of continuous maps of the real line and its converse; and the principal
structure theorem for locally compact abelian groups, with its form for connected groups; and, added in 0.2.2: the
Hahn–Mazurkiewicz theorem (the Hausdorff continuous images of `[0, 1]` are exactly the compact, connected, locally
connected metrizable spaces); Brouwer's characterization of the Cantor set; every infinite totally disconnected compact
group is homeomorphic to a Cantor cube `{0, 1}^κ`; and every compact group is homeomorphic to the product of its
identity component and the quotient by it. To check what is claimed, read this file. `Solution_Topology.lean` proves the same statements from
the library, and `lake comparator` (configured by `comparator/Topology.json`) checks that each proved statement is
exactly the one here and uses only the standard axioms `propext`, `Classical.choice` and `Quot.sound`.

Each statement is the library's statement, copied. The statements were not yet reviewed by a human (see the README).

## Definitions used

Besides Mathlib (`Function.minimalPeriod`, `unitInterval`, `AddCircle`, `Circle`, `PontryaginDual`, `StoneCech`,
`cantorSet`, `Subgroup.connectedComponentOfOne`), the
statements use these definitions of the library (namespace `UndergradKB.Topology`); a reader checking a statement
should check these too:

* `Wallman X`: the closed-set ultrafilters of `X` (families of closed sets with the finite intersection property,
  maximal among such families), with the topology generated by the sets `{u | some F ∈ u lies in O}` for `O` open;
  `Wallman.pt x` is the ultrafilter of the closed sets containing `x` (for `X` a T1 space).
* `DualityHolds G`: there is an isomorphism of topological groups `e : G ≃ₜ* PontryaginDual (PontryaginDual G)` with
  `e g χ = χ g` for all `g` and `χ`, i.e. the canonical map to the double dual is an isomorphism.
* `SharkovskiiLT n k`: `k` comes after `n` in the Sharkovskii order `3 ▹ 5 ▹ 7 ▹ ⋯ ▹ 2·3 ▹ 2·5 ▹ ⋯ ▹ 2²·3 ▹ ⋯ ▹ 2³ ▹
  2² ▹ 2 ▹ 1`. Writing `n = 2ᵃ·m` and `k = 2ᵇ·l` with `m`, `l` odd, it holds when `m > 1`, `l > 1` and either `a < b`
  or (`a = b` and `m < l`); or when `m > 1` and `l = 1`; or when `m = l = 1` and `b < a`. (Meant for positive `n`, `k`.)
-/

namespace UndergradKB.Headlines.Topology

universe u

open Function Set Topology UndergradKB.Topology

/-- **The period three theorem** (Morris Theorem A3.6.2): a continuous map of the real line with a point of
least period 3 has points of every least period `n ≥ 1`. Related: neither Mathlib nor Tau Ceti has it. -/
theorem period_three {f : ℝ → ℝ} (hf : Continuous f)
    (h3 : ∃ a, minimalPeriod f a = 3) : ∀ n : ℕ, 0 < n → ∃ x, minimalPeriod f x = n := by
  sorry

/-- **Peano's space-filling curve** (Morris Theorem 9.5.9): for every `n ≥ 1` there is a continuous map of
`[0, 1]` onto the cube `[0, 1]ⁿ`. Related: Mathlib has the Hausdorff–Alexandroff theorem (every compact metric space
is a continuous image of the Cantor space), not this; Tau Ceti has neither. -/
theorem peano_curve (n : ℕ) (_hn : 0 < n) :
    ∃ ψ : unitInterval → (Fin n → unitInterval), Continuous ψ ∧ Function.Surjective ψ := by
  sorry

/-- **The Banach–Mazur theorem** (Morris Remark 12.2.31): every separable real normed space has a linear isometric
embedding into the space `C([0, 1], ℝ)` of continuous functions with the supremum norm. Related: Mathlib has the
ingredients (Hahn–Banach, Banach–Alaoglu, Hausdorff–Alexandroff); Tau Ceti has the Banach–Mazur distance, a
different notion. -/
theorem banach_mazur (E : Type*) [NormedAddCommGroup E] [NormedSpace ℝ E]
    [TopologicalSpace.SeparableSpace E] : Nonempty (E →ₗᵢ[ℝ] C(unitInterval, ℝ)) := by
  sorry

/-- **Closed subgroups of `ℝⁿ`** (Morris Theorem A5.5.13): a closed subgroup of a finite-dimensional real normed
space is isomorphic as a topological group to `ℝᵖ × ℤ^q`. Related: Mathlib has lattices and the closed subgroups of
`ℝ`; Tau Ceti has density criteria for subgroups of `ℝⁿ`; neither has this classification. -/
theorem closed_subgroup_equiv {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [NormedSpace ℝ E] [FiniteDimensional ℝ E]
    (G : AddSubgroup E) (hG : IsClosed (G : Set E)) :
    ∃ p q : ℕ, Nonempty (G ≃ₜ+ ((Fin p → ℝ) × (Fin q → ℤ))) := by
  sorry

/-- **Quotients of `ℝⁿ` by closed subgroups** (Morris Theorem A5.5.13): the quotient of a finite-dimensional real
normed space by a closed subgroup is isomorphic as a topological group to `ℝᶜ × 𝕋ᵈ`. Related: as above. -/
theorem quotient_closed_subgroup_equiv {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [NormedSpace ℝ E]
    [FiniteDimensional ℝ E] (G : AddSubgroup E) (hG : IsClosed (G : Set E)) :
    ∃ c d : ℕ, Nonempty ((E ⧸ G) ≃ₜ+ ((Fin c → ℝ) × (Fin d → AddCircle (1 : ℝ)))) := by
  sorry

/-- **Closed subgroups of the torus** (Morris Corollary A5.5.20): a closed subgroup `H` of `𝕋ⁿ = (ℝ/ℤ)ⁿ` is
isomorphic as a topological group to `𝕋ᵃ × F` for a finite subgroup `F` of `H`. Related: as above. -/
theorem closed_subgroup_torus_equiv {n : ℕ} (H : AddSubgroup (Fin n → AddCircle (1 : ℝ)))
    (hH : IsClosed (H : Set (Fin n → AddCircle (1 : ℝ)))) :
    ∃ (a : ℕ) (F : AddSubgroup H), Finite F ∧
      Nonempty (H ≃ₜ+ ((Fin a → AddCircle (1 : ℝ)) × F)) := by
  sorry

/-- **The Wallman compactification** (Morris Theorem A6.4.6): for a T1 space `X`, the Wallman space is a compact
T1 space containing `X` as a dense subspace, and every continuous map from `X` to a compact Hausdorff space extends
continuously to it. Related: Mathlib has the Stone–Čech compactification; neither Mathlib nor Tau Ceti has the
Wallman compactification. -/
theorem wallman_compactification (X : Type*) [TopologicalSpace X] [T1Space X] :
    CompactSpace (Wallman X) ∧ T1Space (Wallman X) ∧ Topology.IsEmbedding (Wallman.pt : X → Wallman X) ∧
      DenseRange (Wallman.pt : X → Wallman X) ∧
      ∀ (K : Type _) [TopologicalSpace K] [CompactSpace K] [T2Space K] (f : X → K),
        Continuous f → ∃ g : Wallman X → K, Continuous g ∧ ∀ x, g (Wallman.pt x) = f x := by
  sorry

/-- **The Wallman compactification is the Stone–Čech compactification exactly for normal spaces** (Morris
Corollary A6.4.8): a T1 space `X` is normal if and only if there is a homeomorphism from its Wallman space to its
Stone–Čech compactification compatible with the maps from `X`. Related: as above. -/
theorem normalSpace_iff_wallman_eq_stoneCech {X : Type*} [TopologicalSpace X] [T1Space X] :
    NormalSpace X ↔ ∃ e : Wallman X ≃ₜ StoneCech X, ∀ x, e (Wallman.pt x) = stoneCechUnit x := by
  sorry

/-- **Pontryagin–van Kampen duality for `ℝᵃ × 𝕋ᵇ × ℤᶜ`** (Morris Corollary A5.9.4, the case without a finite
factor): the canonical map from such a group to its double dual is an isomorphism of topological groups (the groups
are written multiplicatively, as Mathlib's `PontryaginDual` requires; `Circle` is the unit circle of `ℂ`). Related:
Mathlib has the Pontryagin dual and duality for finite abelian groups; Tau Ceti computes the dual of `ℤ` and the
characters of tori; neither has the double-dual isomorphism for these groups. -/
theorem pontryagin_duality_real_circle_int (a b c : ℕ) :
    DualityHolds (((Fin a → Multiplicative ℝ) × (Fin b → Circle)) × (Fin c → Multiplicative ℤ)) := by
  sorry

/-- **Pontryagin–van Kampen duality** (Morris Theorem A5.13.6): for every locally compact Hausdorff abelian group
`G`, the canonical map from `G` to its double dual is an isomorphism of topological groups (the group is written
multiplicatively, as Mathlib's `PontryaginDual` requires; the dual carries the compact-open topology). Related:
Mathlib has the Pontryagin dual and duality for finite abelian groups only; Tau Ceti identifies the dual of `ℤ` with
the circle but has no double-dual isomorphism. This strengthens
`pontryagin_duality_real_circle_int`. -/
theorem pontryagin_van_kampen {G : Type u} [CommGroup G] [TopologicalSpace G] [IsTopologicalGroup G]
    [T2Space G] [LocallyCompactSpace G] : DualityHolds G := by
  sorry

/-- **Sharkovskii's theorem** (Morris Theorem A3.6.4): if a continuous map `f : ℝ → ℝ` has a point of least period
`n` and `k` comes after `n` in the Sharkovskii order, then `f` has a point of least period `k`. Related: neither
Mathlib nor Tau Ceti has it; the period three theorem `period_three` is the case `n = 3`. -/
theorem sharkovskii {f : ℝ → ℝ} (hf : Continuous f) {n : ℕ} (hn : 0 < n)
    (hper : ∃ x, minimalPeriod f x = n) {k : ℕ} (hk : 0 < k) (hlt : SharkovskiiLT n k) :
    ∃ y, minimalPeriod f y = k := by
  sorry

/-- **The converse of Sharkovskii's theorem** (Morris Theorem A3.6.6): for every `n ≥ 1` there is a continuous map
`f : ℝ → ℝ` with a point of least period `n` and no point of least period `l` for any `l` that precedes `n` in the
Sharkovskii order. Related: as above. -/
theorem converse_sharkovskii {n : ℕ} (hn : 0 < n) :
    ∃ f : ℝ → ℝ, Continuous f ∧ (∃ x, minimalPeriod f x = n) ∧
      ∀ l, 0 < l → SharkovskiiLT l n → ¬ ∃ x, minimalPeriod f x = l := by
  sorry

/-- **The principal structure theorem for locally compact abelian groups** (Morris Theorem A5.13.8): every locally
compact Hausdorff abelian group `G` has an open subgroup which is isomorphic, as a topological group, to `ℝᵃ × K`
for some `a` and some compact Hausdorff abelian group `K` (the group is written multiplicatively; `K` lives in the
same universe as `G`). Related: Mathlib has Haar measure on locally compact groups but no structure theory of
locally compact abelian groups; Tau Ceti has none either. -/
theorem lca_principal_structure {G : Type u} [CommGroup G] [TopologicalSpace G] [IsTopologicalGroup G]
    [T2Space G] [LocallyCompactSpace G] :
    ∃ S : Subgroup G, IsOpen (S : Set G) ∧
      ∃ (a : ℕ) (K : Type u) (_ : CommGroup K) (_ : TopologicalSpace K),
        IsTopologicalGroup K ∧ CompactSpace K ∧ T2Space K ∧
          Nonempty (S ≃ₜ* (Fin a → Multiplicative ℝ) × K) := by
  sorry

/-- **Structure of connected locally compact abelian groups** (Morris Theorems A5.13.9 and A5.0.20): a connected
locally compact Hausdorff abelian group `G` is isomorphic, as a topological group, to `ℝᵃ × K` for some `a` and some
compact connected Hausdorff abelian group `K`. Related: as above. -/
theorem lca_connected_structure {G : Type u} [CommGroup G] [TopologicalSpace G] [IsTopologicalGroup G]
    [T2Space G] [LocallyCompactSpace G] [ConnectedSpace G] :
    ∃ (a : ℕ) (K : Type u) (_ : CommGroup K) (_ : TopologicalSpace K),
      IsTopologicalGroup K ∧ CompactSpace K ∧ T2Space K ∧ ConnectedSpace K ∧
        Nonempty (G ≃ₜ* (Fin a → Multiplicative ℝ) × K) := by
  sorry

/-- **The Hahn–Mazurkiewicz theorem** (Morris Theorem 9.5.12, both directions): a Hausdorff space is a continuous
image of `[0, 1]` if and only if it is compact, connected, locally connected and metrizable. Related: Mathlib has
Peano-type surjections only from the Cantor space onto compact metric spaces; neither Mathlib nor Tau Ceti has this
theorem. -/
theorem hahn_mazurkiewicz {X : Type*} [TopologicalSpace X] [T2Space X] :
    (∃ f : unitInterval → X, Continuous f ∧ Function.Surjective f) ↔
      CompactSpace X ∧ ConnectedSpace X ∧ LocallyConnectedSpace X ∧
        TopologicalSpace.MetrizableSpace X := by
  sorry

/-- **Brouwer's characterization of the Cantor set** (Morris Exercises 9.1 #2; used for Theorem A5.0.23): every nonempty
compact metrizable totally disconnected space without isolated points is homeomorphic to the Cantor set. Related:
Mathlib proves that the Cantor set is homeomorphic to `ℕ → Bool` (`cantorSetHomeomorphNatToBool`), not this
characterization; Tau Ceti does not have it. -/
theorem brouwer_cantor_set {X : Type*} [TopologicalSpace X] [CompactSpace X]
    [TopologicalSpace.MetrizableSpace X] [TotallyDisconnectedSpace X] [Nonempty X]
    (hper : ∀ x : X, ¬ IsOpen ({x} : Set X)) : Nonempty (X ≃ₜ cantorSet) := by
  sorry

/-- **Totally disconnected compact groups are Cantor cubes** (Morris Theorem A5.0.23): an infinite compact Hausdorff
totally disconnected group is homeomorphic to `{0, 1}^κ` for an infinite set `κ` (no metrizability assumed). Related:
neither Mathlib nor Tau Ceti has it. -/
theorem compact_totally_disconnected_group_cantor_cube {G : Type u} [Group G] [TopologicalSpace G]
    [IsTopologicalGroup G] [CompactSpace G] [T2Space G] [TotallyDisconnectedSpace G] [Infinite G] :
    ∃ κ : Type u, Infinite κ ∧ Nonempty (G ≃ₜ (κ → Bool)) := by
  sorry

/-- **A compact group is its identity component times a totally disconnected group, as a space** (Morris Theorem
A5.0.26): a compact Hausdorff group `G` is homeomorphic to `G₀ × G/G₀`, where `G₀` is the connected component of the
identity (the proof constructs a continuous cross-section of `G → G/G₀`). Related: Mathlib defines `G₀`
(`Subgroup.connectedComponentOfOne`); neither Mathlib nor Tau Ceti has this decomposition. -/
theorem compact_group_homeomorph_identityComponent_prod (G : Type*) [Group G] [TopologicalSpace G]
    [IsTopologicalGroup G] [CompactSpace G] [T2Space G] :
    have := normal_connectedComponentOfOne G
    Nonempty (G ≃ₜ (Subgroup.connectedComponentOfOne G) ×
      (G ⧸ Subgroup.connectedComponentOfOne G)) := by
  sorry

end UndergradKB.Headlines.Topology
